.TH tkucalc n 0.2 tkutils
.SH tkutils::tkucalc
.PP
A small desktop calculator widget: a display plus a button grid, with keyboard support. A namespaced, reusable replacement for ad\-hoc global\-variable calculator scripts. Pure Tk + \fBexpr\fR; nothing external. Tk 8.6+ and 9.x.
.SS API
.CS
::tkutils::tkucalc::widget   path ?-onresult script?
::tkutils::tkucalc::evaluate text     ;# the arithmetic core (no GUI)
::tkutils::tkucalc::clear    path     ;# clear the display
::tkutils::tkucalc::getHistory   path ;# past "expr = result" lines
::tkutils::tkucalc::clearHistory path ;# forget the history
.CE
.IP \(bu
\fB\-onresult\fR if given, is appended the result string after each successful \fB=\fR.
.IP \(bu
\fB\-history\fR show a history panel (a listbox of past "expr = result" lines); double\-click or Return on a line loads its result back into the display.
.SS "The arithmetic core"
.PP
\fBevaluate\fR turns a display expression into a number, independent of the GUI, so it is easy to test and reuse:
.CS
::tkutils::tkucalc::evaluate "2+3*4"    ;# -> 14
::tkutils::tkucalc::evaluate "10/4"     ;# -> 2.5   (division is floating-point)
::tkutils::tkucalc::evaluate "2^10"     ;# -> 1024  (^ is power)
::tkutils::tkucalc::evaluate "3,5 + 1"  ;# -> 4.5   (German decimal comma)
::tkutils::tkucalc::evaluate "7 % 3"    ;# -> 1     (modulo)
.CE
.PP
It accepts the on\-screen math symbols (x, /, \-) and the German decimal comma, allows only a whitelist of characters (digits, \fB+ \- * / ( ) . ^ %\fR, exponent \fBe\fR), and evaluates in a \fBsafe interpreter\fR \-\- so a hand\-typed expression can never run commands or touch the file system. Division uses floating point (\fB10/4\fR is \fB2.5\fR, not integer \fB2\fR), whole results drop a trailing \fB.0\fR, and division by zero, letters, brackets, or variable references raise an error.
.SS Widget
.CS
package require tkutils::tkucalc
::tkutils::tkucalc::widget .calc
pack .calc
.CE
.PP
Buttons: digits \fB0\fR\-\fB9\fR, \fB.\fR, operators \fB+ \- x /\fR, parentheses, \fBC\fR (clear), \fB<\fR (backspace) and \fB=\fR (evaluate). \fBKeyboard\fR works throughout: the display is a normal entry, so type digits, operators and parentheses directly; Enter evaluates, Escape clears, BackSpace deletes. Buttons do not steal keyboard focus, so you can freely mix clicking and typing. A bad expression shows \fBError\fR instead of crashing.
.PP
With \fB\-history 1\fR a panel below the keypad lists past calculations; double\-click a line to reuse its result:
.CS
::tkutils::tkucalc::widget .calc -history 1
.CE
.CS
# feed each result somewhere:
::tkutils::tkucalc::widget .calc -onresult {apply {{r} {puts "= $r"}}}
.CE
.SS "See also"
.PP
\fBtkunumentry\fR, \fBtkuvalidate\fR
